  {
    "schema_version": "1.0",
    "page_id": "topic-2-10-7",
    "page_path": "/explore/culture/contemporary-everyday-culture/changing-social-values/",
    "name": "Changing Social Values",
    "level": 3,
    "parent_path": "/explore/culture/contemporary-everyday-culture/",
    "entity_type": "concept",
    "address": null,
    "phone": null,
    "email": null,
    "website": null,
    "logo": null,
    "contact_source_ids": [],
    "description": "Social values in Iran are studied through surveys, demographic research, and evidence about public behavior. These sources illuminate different aspects of change and should not be combined into a single claim that all Iranians are moving in the same direction.",
    "tags": ["Social change", "Public opinion", "Family", "Gender", "Survey methods"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T13:41:32Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T13:41:32Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Changing Social Values in Iran: Evidence and Interpretation | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Understand what surveys, family research and public behavior can—and cannot—reveal about changing values in Iranian society."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "reading-change",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Change requires more than a striking example",
        "content": "A claim about changing values needs a baseline: whose attitudes, toward which question, measured when? Syracuse University's Iran Data Portal brings together public-opinion surveys concerning cultural preferences, social attitudes, and other subjects across pre-revolutionary and post-revolutionary periods. Its collection makes historical comparison possible in principle, but the presence of several surveys does not establish that their questions or samples are interchangeable. [s1]\n\nThe distinction between values, circumstances, and behavior is essential. A person may want a particular family life yet postpone it because of economic insecurity. Someone may comply publicly with a rule while disagreeing privately. Conversely, a conspicuous act of defiance does not reveal how common that attitude is nationally. These are interpretive safeguards for reading the survey, demographic, and public-behavior evidence discussed below. [s1][s3][s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s3", "s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "survey-evidence",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What surveys can establish",
        "content": "The World Values Survey is an international research program that investigates beliefs and attitudes through standardized questionnaires. Its seventh-wave documentation describes nationwide probability sampling. The Iran study is catalogued as a 2020 survey, with separate questionnaire, sample-design, and methodology documents. These are the materials needed to determine what a published result actually represents. [s2][s5]\n\nRepeated survey waves can help distinguish an apparent national shift from differences between groups, but only after checking wording, response categories, coverage, and weighting. A survey conducted in one year is a snapshot, not an annual trend. Differences between younger and older respondents also do not, by themselves, prove that the same individuals changed their minds as they aged. This page therefore does not convert isolated survey entries into a year-by-year narrative of liberalization, conservatism, or declining trust. [s1][s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s5"]
      },
      {
        "id": "family-aspirations",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Family decisions combine aspirations and constraints",
        "content": "Research on marriage and fertility provides a useful warning against explaining behavior through values alone. The accessible abstract of a 2024 study by Meimanat Hosseini-Chavoshi, Peter McDonald, and Mohammad Jalal Abbasi-Shavazi discusses marriage patterns, economic conditions, and the design of policies intended to encourage births. The authors argue that policy incentives do not closely match younger people's aspirations. This is the researchers' interpretation, not a direct measure of a single national youth outlook. [s3]\n\nThe practical lesson is to ask separate questions: what family arrangements people prefer, what they can afford, and what they eventually do. Falling births or delayed parenthood cannot automatically be translated into rejection of family life. The study's abstract supports the importance of economic and marriage-related explanations; detailed assessment of its estimates requires inspection of the full paper. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "public-autonomy",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Public autonomy and unequal exposure",
        "content": "Disputes over compulsory hijab—the legally required head covering—show how social values can become questions of public authority. In March 2025, a United Nations fact-finding mission described continued prosecution of women who defied the requirement, despite political assurances about easing enforcement. It also reported particular targeting of ethnic and religious minorities in the protest context. These findings document conflict and unequal exposure to state action; they are not a representative attitude survey. [s4]\n\nA careful account therefore separates legal rules, enforcement, personal belief, and observable conduct. It also leaves room for disagreement within generations, families, and communities. The evidence assembled here establishes useful ways to interpret change, but does not yet establish the direction or magnitude of national changes in religiosity, interpersonal trust, gender attitudes, or intergenerational obligations.",
        "source_ids": ["s4"]
      }
    ],
