    "schema_version": "1.0",
    "page_id": "topic-3",
    "page_path": "/explore/geography/",
    "name": "Nature & Geography",
    "level": 1,
    "parent_path": "/explore/",
    "entity_type": "category",
    "address": null,
    "phone": null,
    "email": null,
    "website": null,
    "logo": null,
    "contact_source_ids": [],
    "description": "Iran's landscapes combine mountain systems, interior basins, contrasting coastlands, forests, and drylands. Relief and water connect these environments, shaping habitats, settlement, and the environmental questions explored throughout this guide.",
    "tags": ["Physical geography", "Mountains", "Water", "Forests", "Deserts", "Biodiversity", "Human geography"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T13:41:32Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T13:41:32Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Nature & Geography of Iran: Mountains, Coasts and Drylands | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Discover Iran through its mountain systems, interior basins, Caspian forests, southern coasts and the relationships between land, water and settlement."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "landscape-framework",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Begin with relief, not a single landscape",
        "content": "Iran is easier to understand as a connected set of contrasting environments than as a uniformly dry country. Mountain chains enclose interior basins, while northern and southern coastlands introduce different conditions at the country's edges. The Iranian highlands also extend beyond modern borders: a physical region and a political territory are not the same thing. This distinction helps explain why a map of mountain systems tells a different story from a map of provinces. [s1]\n\nA useful starting framework separates the Zagros Mountains in the west, the Alborz Mountains in the north, the central plateau, the Caspian coastal plain, and the Khuzestan and southern coastal plains. An Iranian soil-management presentation hosted by the Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations uses these broad physiographic units. They are an orientation tool rather than rigid ecological compartments; rivers, slopes, and transitional habitats connect them. [s2]\n\nThe Physical Geography branch develops this framework through individual ranges, basins, and plains. Its purpose is to explain the shape of the country before turning to particular ecosystems or contemporary pressures.",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "mountains-climate",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Mountains organize climatic contrasts",
        "content": "The Alborz demonstrates how strongly relief can divide neighboring environments. It separates the Caspian lowlands from the interior uplands, and its northern and southern slopes differ sharply in climate and vegetation. Moist air reaching the Caspian-facing slopes supports deciduous forest, while the inland side is substantially drier. The range therefore functions as an ecological barrier as well as a prominent feature on the skyline. [s3]\n\nThis contrast cautions against applying a national climate description to every locality. A coastal plain, a high mountain valley, and a basin behind a mountain barrier may lie relatively close together yet have very different moisture and temperature conditions. The Climate & Weather branch should be read alongside Mountains & Highlands: elevation, exposure, and position relative to moist air are essential parts of the explanation. [s3]\n\nThe Zagros forms another major organizing system, extending through western and southern Iran. Geological accounts distinguish it from the Alborz rather than treating all Iranian mountains as one uniform structure. Its relationship with the plateau and southern lowlands belongs within a wider regional framework that also includes the Makran ranges. [s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s3", "s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "water-basins",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Follow water across the landscape",
        "content": "Water provides a second way to read the country. Mountains, lowlands, and interior basins create different settings for water resources and their use. FAO's AQUASTAT country profile treats geography and climate together with water resources, irrigation, drainage, management, and environmental conditions. That organization is useful: the presence of water, access to it, and the way it is managed are related but distinct questions. [s5]\n\nThe Rivers & Water Resources branch examines named rivers and infrastructure, while Seas & Lakes introduces major receiving waters and inland lakes. These branches should not be reduced to a catalogue of scenic features. A river is part of a catchment, and a lake's condition requires evidence about the system supplying it. Current claims about water availability also need current observations; an older national profile cannot establish today's storage or flow. [s5]\n\nCoastlands add another contrast. Iran faces the Caspian Sea to the north and the Persian Gulf and Gulf of Oman to the south. Their different positions within the country's surrounding geography make Islands & Coasts a distinct subject rather than an extension of the interior desert story. [s6]",
        "source_ids": ["s5", "s6"]
      },
      {
        "id": "drylands-process",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Drylands are active landscapes",
        "content": "The Lut Desert illustrates why desert geography is about processes as well as appearance. UNESCO describes an interior basin surrounded by mountains, where rain-shadow conditions combine with high temperatures to produce extreme aridity. Strong winds move sediment and sculpt yardangs: elongated ridges formed by wind erosion. Stony surfaces and dune fields add further variety. [s7]\n\nA desert is therefore not simply an empty expanse of sand. The Lut's landforms record the interaction of rock, sediment, wind, and basin setting. The Deserts & Drylands branch explores these environments, while Physical Geography explains the broader structures containing them. Keeping both perspectives helps readers distinguish a landform's origin from its present ecological condition. [s7]\n\nRecognition does not remove management problems. UNESCO identifies inappropriate tourism and motorized access as concerns for the Lut property. Its account demonstrates that even a landscape valued for ongoing natural processes requires attention to visitor behavior, surrounding communities, and protection. [s7]",
        "source_ids": ["s7"]
      },
      {
        "id": "forests-biodiversity",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Forests reveal another Iran",
        "content": "Along the southern and southwestern Caspian margins, the Hyrcanian forests present a strikingly different environment. UNESCO describes a broad-leaved forest region extending across Iran and Azerbaijan, with rare and endemic trees and ecosystems that include large predators. The World Heritage property consists of selected components; it is not synonymous with every remaining Hyrcanian forest. [s8]\n\nThis distinction between an ecological region and a designated property is important throughout the guide. A protected boundary identifies a particular management and recognition framework, while habitats and animal movements can extend beyond it. UNESCO specifically emphasizes connectivity between Hyrcanian components and acknowledges that enforcement of access and use restrictions needs strengthening. [s8]\n\nForests & Vegetation introduces the country's different plant environments; Biodiversity turns to species, habitats, and protected areas. Reading them together shifts attention from isolated charismatic animals to the environments and connections on which they depend. The Hyrcanian example makes that relationship especially clear. [s8]",
        "source_ids": ["s8"]
      },
      {
        "id": "people-and-regional-reading",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Connect physical and human geography",
        "content": "Iran's geographic branches are complementary ways of asking questions about the same territory. A geological account distinguishes major natural regions through their underlying structure; a soil-management account emphasizes land capability and agricultural environments. Neither perspective alone explains every aspect of a place. [s2][s4]\n\nHuman Geography adds the distribution of settlement and population to this physical framework. Natural Hazards supplies a separate route into earthquakes, floods, drought, subsidence, landslides, and dust storms, each of which requires its own evidence rather than a single national risk label. These are the subjects organized by this guide's existing taxonomy, not a claim that every location faces equal exposure.\n\nThe most useful habit is to compare scales. Begin with the country's mountain-and-basin framework, move to a coast, catchment, or habitat, and then examine a particular locality. Broad descriptions explain contrasts; local and dated evidence establishes present conditions. Together, these approaches reveal a country whose geography is defined by relationships among terrain, water, ecosystems, and human use. [s1][s5]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s4", "s5"]
      },
      {
        "id": "regional-framework",
        "type": "markdown-mermaid",
        "title": "A broad framework for reading Iran's landscapes",
        "content": "```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n  A[\"Iran's broad physiographic framework\"] -->|\"Northern mountain unit\"| B[\"Alborz Mountains\"]\n  A -->|\"Western mountain unit\"| C[\"Zagros Mountains\"]\n  A -->|\"Interior unit\"| D[\"Central Plateau\"]\n  A -->|\"Northern lowland unit\"| E[\"Caspian Coastal Plain\"]\n  A -->|\"Southern lowland units\"| F[\"Khuzestan and Southern Coastal Plains\"]\n```",
        "summary": "The arrows classify broad physiographic units used in the inspected soil-management presentation. They do not represent drainage directions, administrative borders, or sharp ecological boundaries.",
        "as_of_date": null,
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      }
    ],
